          title: "He touched him — the contagion runs backwards",
          body: "Under the law a leper was untouchable in the strictest sense: he lived “outside the camp,” cried *unclean, unclean* at anyone's approach (Lev 13:45-46), and his touch defiled. A word would have sufficed — the centurion two paragraphs later gets exactly that. Jesus **touches him first, and heals him second**; the sentence order is deliberate, and no impurity is recorded as transferring. Matthew is dramatizing a rule that runs through this whole Gospel: contact with Jesus reverses the direction of contagion — holiness proves more communicable than defilement. The man's opening is worth hearing too: *“if you will”* — he doubts the willingness, never the power. The answer he gets, **θέλω** (“I will”), is one word long, and it is the answer this Gospel gives to everyone who wonders whether they, specifically, would be wanted back.",
