      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a8.10.1',
          kind: "concept",
          refs: ['g8.10.5', 'g8.10.16'],
          lemma: "ἐθαύμασεν … τοσαύτην πίστιν",
          title: "The only thing that ever amazed him",
          body: "Θαυμάζω — to marvel, be astonished — is what crowds do at Jesus all through the Gospels. Only twice in the whole tradition does the verb run the other way, with *Jesus* as its subject: here, at a pagan officer's **faith**, and in Mark 6:6, at his hometown's **unbelief**. Miracles never amaze him; armies wouldn't; the two things that do are trust and its absence — as if, from his side of the exchange, faith were the genuinely surprising phenomenon in the world. And what is the faith that stuns him? Nothing mystical: a soldier's inference that authority does not require presence — a piece of *reasoning about how commands work*, extended to God. It is one of several places where the Gospels' “faith” looks less like a feeling and more like a conclusion someone dares to act on. That the concluder is a gentile occupier — the fourth outsider so honoured since the magi — triggers vv. 11-12: the banquet's seating chart is about to astonish everyone.",
        },
      ],
