      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a8.17.1',
          kind: "concept",
          refs: ['g8.17.14', 'g8.17.18'],
          lemma: "ἔλαβεν … ἐβάστασεν",
          title: "Isaiah 53, quoted about medicine",
          body: "The line is from the fourth Servant song — *“surely he has borne our griefs and carried our sorrows”* (Isa 53:4) — the passage Christians read above all at the cross. Matthew quotes it here, about an evening clinic in Capernaum, and translates the Hebrew freshly (not via the Septuagint, which spiritualizes to “sins”): the Servant carries **weaknesses and diseases**, bodily ones. Two things follow. Healing, for Matthew, is not a marketing miracle bolted onto the message; it *is* the Servant's work in progress — the bearing has already begun, one fever at a time. And the citation quietly links the clinic to the cross: the same shoulders. Whatever a modern reader concludes about individual healing stories, Matthew's claim about where God stands relative to human pain is unambiguous — not above it, dispensing; under it, carrying.",
        },
      ],
