      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a8.26.1',
          kind: "concept",
          refs: ['g8.24.3', 'g8.26.10', 'g8.27.17'],
          lemma: "σεισμὸς … ἐπετίμησεν",
          title: "Rebuking the sea — and the question the boat is left with",
          body: "Matthew's storm-word is not the usual one: **σεισμός**, an *earthquake* of water — the term he reuses at the crucifixion and the empty tomb, marking scenes where something structural gives way. And Jesus **rebukes** (ἐπιτιμάω) the wind and sea, the verb used for silencing demons — behind which stands the Old Testament's oldest battle-imagery: the LORD who rebukes and dries the sea, who stills its raging (Ps 106:9; 89:9), chaos-water being the Bible's default emblem of everything untameable. Only God does that in those texts; hence the disciples' closing question, which Matthew leaves deliberately unanswered in the boat: *“What sort of man is this…?”* Note also the scolding's shape: “little-faiths” — not *no*-faiths; they did, after all, wake him and ask. Panicked prayer to a sleeping God is still, this scene insists on recording, prayer that gets answered.",
        },
      ],
