          lemma: "παρεκάλεσαν ὅπως μεταβῇ",
          title: "The town that asked him to leave",
          body: "The chapter's last miracle gets the strangest reception in it. A road made impassable by two violent men is now safe; the men are sane; and the town's unanimous response is a petition — *please go away*. Matthew offers no editorial, but the arithmetic is visible: the deliverance cost a herd of pigs, and a countable loss in livestock weighed more than an uncountable gain in persons. (Gadara was Decapolis country, gentile territory — the pigs were legal, valuable, and somebody's livelihood; the fear is not irrational, which is what makes it damning.) It is an early sighting of a pattern the Gospels return to: the actual obstacle to welcoming what heals is rarely intellectual doubt — nobody in Gadara disputes the miracle — but the suspicion that restoration will be *expensive*, that a healed world might cost us our current arrangements. The town believed perfectly. It just preferred the demons at the road's edge to a Lord in the ledger. He granted the request, notes the next verse, and got into the boat; this Gospel's Jesus does not stay where he is asked to leave — a severity worth pondering longer than the pigs.",
